module dev.slatelang.slate

import sh.sysl.gc
import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}
import sysl.container.{Map, map}
import sysl.math.bigint.{BigInt, zero as big_zero}

// The collected heap, and the objects in it.
//
// **This file is the objects and how one is made**: the heap a VM allocates in, what a cell owns
// outside it, the object types, the reserves, the allocator, the constructors and the payload-growing
// mutators. **How an object is traced, finalized and collected is `collector.sysl`** -- the tracers,
// the `gc.Kind` tables, the roots and the shadow stack, and the schedule.
//
// **Reference counting cannot free a cycle, and slate makes one on every named function.** `define`
// binds a closure into the very scope that closure captured, so the scope holds the closure and the
// closure holds the scope. Nothing declared that back-edge, so `weak` cannot help: it is a cycle in
// somebody else's program, which is the case `sh.sysl.gc` exists for.
//
// ## What is collected and what is not
//
// **The collector manages exactly what refcounting cannot.** A string, an array, an object, a
// closure and a scope are collected, because a value graph made of them can cycle. The *tree* is
// not: an `Expr` is immutable and acyclic, so ARC frees it correctly and it stays where it is.
//
// A slate string is a GC object holding a sysl `string`, which its finalizer releases -- so a string
// takes part in the collector's lifetime the way Go's does, without this file reimplementing every
// string operation over raw bytes. The same bargain gives an array its `Buf` and an object its
// `Map`.
//
// **`Value` therefore holds no reference-counted member at all**: scalars and raw pointers, nothing
// else. That is what lets one be stored into GC memory, copied into a `Buf`, and passed around with
// no ARC traffic on the interpreter's hot path.

// How many bytes the collected heap gets by default.
//
// One block, fixed when the VM is made -- there is no growing, and `alloc` answers null when it is
// full. **A VM asks for its own ceiling** (`new_vm(ceiling)` in `vm_state.sysl`, which `malloc`s the
// block), and this is what the program's own VM asks for, so the default behaviour is what it was.
//
// **Four megabytes was the first number and it was too small to build anything with**: about twelve
// thousand objects with a proto, fewer than a program reading a modest CSV would make. This holds
// something over four hundred thousand.
//
// **THE SIZE USED TO COST BUILD TIME, AND THAT WAS A SYSL DEFECT RATHER THAN A FACT ABOUT SLATE.**
// Compiling this file once took time proportional to the size of the array -- 12 s at 4 MiB, 116 s at
// 64, and 256 MiB never finished -- though the array is zero-initialised and the linker emits it as
// BSS, so the binary never grew. `StaticEmitter.constantValue` lowered `[0; N]` as `List.fill(N)` of
// the element constant, handing LLVM an N-element array literal where `zeroinitializer` says the same
// thing in one word.
//
// **Fixed in sysl 0.0.86, card 0319, and confirmed from this end.** The IR is now flat: 5,129,651
// bytes at 4 MiB against 5,129,657 at 256 MiB, a difference of six -- the digits in the number. The
// global reads `[268435456 x i8] zeroinitializer` and the whole build takes ten seconds.
//
// **It reproduced only on MODULE storage.** The same declaration inside a function is a body local and
// lowers to an `alloca` and a fill loop, flat at every size -- sysl's first reduction was a single
// entry file and showed nothing, which nearly sent the diagnosis the other way. The arena was a
// module-level `[HeapBytes]u8` for exactly that reason and is a `malloc` now, so none of this binds
// any more; it is kept because the sysl defect is worth knowing about before somebody writes the
// next big array.
//
// **A `malloc`ed block is NOT zero-filled and nothing here needs it to be**: `gc.alloc` zeroes every
// payload before the caller sees it, which its own doc says is what makes storing a reference-counted
// member safe. **`malloc` rather than `calloc` is the whole of what keeps the old BSS bargain** --
// the pages cost address space and are committed by the operating system only as they are touched,
// so a program that uses a megabyte still pays for a megabyte rather than for 256 of them.
// Not `private`: `new_vm` in `vm_state.sysl` gives the program's own VM this ceiling.
const HeapBytes: usize = 268435456

// The heap this line of execution allocates in.
//
// **A `*gc.Heap` accessor rather than a global, which is the `machine()`/`running_unit()` shape**, so
// every site below reads `slate_heap()` where it read the global, and nothing else moved. The block
// under it is `current().region`, `malloc`ed by `new_vm` and given back by `dispose_vm`.
slate_heap() -> *gc.Heap = &current().heap

// How much of that block a program may actually have.
//
// **`current().heap_ceiling` is the block and `current().heap_limit` is the setting**, which lets a test
// about running out of memory run out of a small one. Filling 256 MiB to find out what happens at the
// end of it takes nearly five seconds; filling one takes no time at all, and the behaviour under test
// is the same either way. Nothing but the suite changes it, and `run` puts it back.

// Give the next program a heap of this size. `reset_heap` is what acts on it.
// **The ceiling is this VM's own block and not `HeapBytes`**, which is the whole of what a per-VM
// region changed here: a VM spawned with a megabyte cannot be asked for more than a megabyte, where
// the shared arena would have handed it 256.
// **A named limit below the block bounds PAYLOAD too**, which is what `Vm.payload_capped` says and
// is the whole of how a test measures that bound: the program's own VM is uncapped by default, and
// putting the limit back to the block puts that default back with it.
set_heap_limit(bytes: usize)
    val v = current()

    v.heap_limit = if bytes > v.heap_ceiling then v.heap_ceiling else bytes
    v.payload_capped = v.heap_limit < v.heap_ceiling

// -- what a cell owns outside the heap ------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **A cell is small and what it owns is not**, so a heap measured in cells alone says almost nothing
// about what a program is holding: a one-megabyte byte buffer is a `BytesObj` of a few words with a
// megabyte of counted storage hanging off it. Neither the collection schedule nor the ceiling could
// see that storage, which made two separate failures out of one hole -- garbage piling up because a
// collection was never due, and an actor outgrowing the heap it named without ever being told.
//
// **`current().payload` is the figure and it is kept two ways at once.** Every site that allocates
// or grows a payload charges it, and every site that hands one back credits it; and because a charge
// without its credit would grow for ever, the mark phase recomputes the whole figure from the live
// objects at every collection. So a missed charge costs a stale schedule until the next collection
// and can never become a false out-of-memory.
//
// **The measure is CAPACITY and not length.** A `Buf` that has grown to a thousand slots is holding
// a thousand slots' worth of storage whatever its count says, and `clear` and `pop` give none of it
// back -- so charging by length would report a buffer a program emptied as costing nothing while the
// pages are still committed.

// Take note of payload just allocated.
charge(n: usize)
    charge_on(current(), n)

// The same, for a caller that already holds the VM. **Every constructor below takes this form**, so
// that one allocation asks for the VM once rather than once for its reserve, once for its heap and
// once for this: `current()` reads a thread-local, which on this platform is a call into the dynamic
// loader and not a load.
charge_on(v: *Vm, n: usize)
    v.payload += n

// Take note of payload just given back. **Clamped at zero**: the figure is an estimate between
// collections, and a credit larger than the running count must not wrap a `usize`.
credit(n: usize)
    val v = current()

    v.payload = if v.payload > n then v.payload - n else 0

// Move the count from one footprint to another, which is what a container that was regrown owes.
recharge(was: usize, now: usize)
    if now > was
        charge(now - was)
    elif was > now
        credit(was - now)

// What the heap and everything hanging off it hold, which is the number both the schedule and the
// ceiling read. **Cells alone is the figure that took a bug to learn was wrong.**
heap_in_use() -> usize = gc.live_size(slate_heap()) + current().payload

// -- the objects --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A slate string. Immutable, as Go's is, so nothing ever writes through one.
//
// **The GC object is the sysl string's ONLY owner, and that is the point of the arrangement.**
// `new_str` takes the string by value and stores it; the caller's copy dies with the caller's frame,
// which drops the count to one -- the one inside this object. From then on nothing but the
// collector decides when those bytes go, and `finalize_str` is what takes the count to zero. A
// caller that squirrels a second copy away somewhere would break exactly that, which is why nothing
// in slate holds a sysl `string` for a slate string value: a `Value` holds a `*StrObj` and nothing
// else.
// **THE CELL CARRIES THE CHARACTER COUNT, AND IT IS NEVER WORKED OUT TWICE.** A slate position is a
// character and the bytes are UTF-8, so `s.length` and `s[i]` used to walk the string every time
// they were asked -- which makes an ordinary left-to-right read quadratic. The count is written when
// the cell is made and a string is immutable, so it can never go stale.
//
// **`chars == s.bytes.len` IS THE TEST FOR "EVERY CHARACTER IS ONE BYTE"**, and that falls out of
// keeping the count rather than costing a flag of its own. For such a string a character position
// *is* a byte offset, so indexing and slicing are arithmetic.
//
// **The cursor is the answer for a string that is NOT all one-byte**: the last position asked about
// and the byte offset it landed on, so a walk forwards or backwards costs the distance from the last
// question rather than the distance from the start. `(0, 0)` is what a zeroed cell holds and is also
// the right answer for character zero, so there is no sentinel and nothing to initialise.
//
// **It is mutable state on an immutable value and it needs no lock.** A string never crosses a
// thread by reference -- an actor message copies every value into the receiving heap -- so the only
// writer of a cell's cursor is the one VM that can reach it.
struct StrObj
    s: string
    chars: usize
    cursor_char: usize
    cursor_byte: usize

    // The shared storage this string's bytes sit at the front of, when it was made by appending
    // (`str_room.sysl`), and null otherwise. Such a string's bytes are charged to the room.
    room: *RoomObj

// A slate integer that has outgrown 64 bits.
//
// **A heap object for `StrObj`'s reason and on exactly its terms.** A `BigInt` is a sign and a view
// of limbs, and a view is reference counted -- so it may not sit in a `Value`, which holds no
// counted member at all. The cell owns it, `finalize_big` gives it back, and what a `Value` carries
// is the pointer.
//
// **A `Big` THAT FITS A `long` NEVER EXISTS.** Every producer goes through `big_value` in
// `value.sysl`, which asks `to_long()` first and answers an `Int` where it fits. That single
// invariant is what keeps `==`, `<` and hashing free of a cross-kind case: two values of the same
// number are the same variant, so `Int(5)` and a big five can never both be in a table.
struct BigObj
    n: BigInt

// A slate array. Its elements are `Value`s, which is what `trace` walks.
struct ArrayObj
    items: Buf[Value]

    // Whether `freeze` has closed this array to writing.
    //
    // **A field for `ObjectObj.frozen`'s reason**, and the array is the kind that needed one most:
    // there is nowhere else to hang the answer, an array having no table to carry a flag in. The
    // mutating natives and `xs[i] = v` are the only readers.
    frozen: bool

// A slate byte buffer: a run of raw bytes, one byte per byte, that a program may grow and write
// through.
//
// **A `Buf[u8]` and not a `Buf[Value]`, and that is the whole reason this kind exists.** An array of
// numbers holding bytes costs a `Value` per byte -- sixteen bytes of storage for one byte of data --
// and every read of one goes through a tagged union. Here a byte is a byte, `length` is `data.len()`,
// and appending a whole buffer to another is one `extend` rather than a loop in the interpreter.
//
// **Nothing in the region is a `Value`, so the tracer walks nothing** -- `trace_bytes` is empty for
// `trace_str`'s reason, and the `Buf` is reference counted outside the collected heap the way a
// string's `string` and an array's `Buf` are, given back by the finalizer.
struct BytesObj
    data: Buf[u8]

    // Whether `freeze` has closed this buffer to writing.
    //
    // **A buffer is a container a program writes through, so it needs the flag exactly as an array
    // does** — leaving it out would make `isFrozen` answer for a kind `freeze` had not closed, which
    // is a lie rather than a gap.
    frozen: bool

// A slate object: a record, keyed by any value at all.
//
// **The layout is `table.sysl`'s compact dict**, so the fields keep the order they were written in
// and the tracer below is a linear walk rather than a walk of somebody else's container.
struct ObjectObj
    // Where the entries are -- the cell's own `small`, or a `malloc`ed spill block once the table has
    // outgrown the cell -- how many that block has room for (0 while the table is in its cell), and
    // how many entries there are wherever they live. Kept by `entries.sysl` alone, so a read is one
    // index off `at` with no question asked about which of the two it is.
    at: *Entry
    spill_cap: usize
    count: usize
    index: Buf[usize]

    // How many entries are not tombstones.
    live: usize

    // Whether this object is closed to writing, whatever closed it.
    //
    // **A field rather than a question asked of the proto chain**, which is what the alternative
    // would have been: a walk on every field write, paid by every object, to answer something that
    // is settled the moment the object is built.
    //
    // **TWO THINGS SET IT AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS A DATA VALUE**, which is why `data_value` sits
    // beside it: a data variant's object is sealed where it is built, and `freeze(o)` closes any
    // object a program hands it. Everything that refuses a WRITE reads this one; everything that
    // asks *what kind of value is this* reads the other.
    frozen: bool

    // Whether this object is a DATA VALUE.
    //
    // **`frozen` says a write is refused and this says WHY, and the two answer different
    // questions.** `variant_name`, `unknown_field_complaint`, the sentence a refused write gets and
    // what `with` copies all read this -- a frozen plain object is not a data value, so `with` on one
    // answers an ordinary unfrozen copy and a field it never declared is not complained about.
    data_value: bool

    // Whether this table holds a `(val)` mark, which a class object does and nothing else.
    //
    // **It is what keeps the `val` refusal off the hot path.** Writing a field the object already
    // has is a store through a remembered position and asks nothing; a class is the one object where
    // that store could land on a `val`, so this flag is what sends a write to that object -- and to
    // no other -- through the slower path that looks.
    has_vals: bool

    // Whether this table finds a key by IDENTITY rather than by what it holds.
    //
    // **A weak map and nothing else**, and it is a field for `frozen`'s reason: it is decided where
    // the table is made and never changes, so a walk asking it per lookup would be paying for an
    // answer that was settled at the allocation. `table.sysl`'s `key_hash` and `keys_match` are the
    // two places it is read.
    by_identity: bool

    // One bit per key this table has ever held, taken from the key's hash.
    //
    // **IT ANSWERS "NO" EXACTLY AND "MAYBE" OTHERWISE, which is what a lookup that is going to MISS
    // wants.** A method call asks the receiver for a name the receiver does not have -- every time,
    // by construction, the method being the class's -- and that question costs a walk of the whole
    // table. One `and` answers it here.
    //
    // **A REMOVED KEY LEAVES ITS BIT BEHIND AND THAT IS DELIBERATE**: the set only ever grows, so
    // the answer is only ever conservative, and the cost of being wrong is the search that would
    // have happened anyway. Clearing a bit would need to know that no other key shares it.
    keys_bloom: u64

    // The entries of a table that fits its cell (`entries.sysl`).
    //
    // **LAST, so every other field is in the cell's first cache line.** A large table never reads
    // these, and put first they pushed the table, the index and the summary two lines further out:
    // `mapset` and `methods`, which read nothing else, were 4-5% slower for it.
    small: [InlineEntries]Entry

// A function and the scope it was written in.
//
// **The scope is the whole of what makes it a closure**, and the reason this file exists: a function
// that outlives the call that made it keeps that scope alive, and a named function's scope holds the
// function.
struct ClosureObj
    params: Buf[string]

    // An index into `bodies`, not an `Expr`: a counted box may not live in raw GC memory.
    body: usize

    scope: *EnvObj

// A value that is not there yet.
//
// **It is a collected object like any other, and it has to be**: a promise outlives the call that
// made it by definition, is held by whoever awaits it *and* by the coroutine that will settle it, and
// those two release it in an order nobody controls.
//
// **What it holds that an object does not is a list of lines of execution.** A machine waiting here
// is parked, and its id is what this keeps -- an index into `parked` rather than a pointer, so
// nothing in the collected heap has to know what a `Machine` is.
struct PromiseObj
    // What it settled to. Meaningless until `settled`, and `Null` when it failed with nothing in it.
    //
    // **A REJECTION THAT CARRIES A VALUE KEEPS IT HERE**, which is what lets `await risky() catch
    // NotFound(id)` sort a fault that crossed a promise by what it was. One slot serves both because
    // a promise settles once and is never both: `thrown` is what says which of the two this is, and
    // `trace_promise` already marks this field whichever it holds.
    value: Value

    settled: bool
    failed: bool

    // Whether the failure carries `value` as the thing a `catch` binds -- see `Signal.Fail`.
    thrown: bool

    // The complaint and where it was raised, for a promise that failed. A `string` lives here the way
    // `StrObj`'s does, with the finalizer below giving it back.
    message: string
    at: Span

    // **Which file `at` is an offset into, carried the way a `Fail` carries its own `src`.** A
    // promise outlives the call that settled it, so the file has to travel with the span rather
    // than being read off whatever the machine happens to be running when the rejection is finally
    // reported -- that mismatch is what sent a fault raised in one file to a byte offset in another.
    src: usize

    // **Whether this promise failed because `exit` was called, and the status it was given.** A
    // rejection that crosses an `await` is otherwise flattened to a message and a span, which loses
    // whether it was `Quit` or an ordinary `Fail` -- and `Quit` has to be told apart from every other
    // failure everywhere it resurfaces, because it is not a diagnostic anywhere but `slate test`; it
    // is the program's own exit.
    quit: bool
    quit_status: long

    // The parked machines to hand the answer to. Emptied as they are queued.
    waiters: Buf[usize]

    // Whether anything has ever awaited this.
    //
    // **This is what tells a rejection that was dealt with from one that vanished.** A promise that
    // fails with nothing waiting on it is the commonest way an async program loses an error -- node
    // calls it an unhandled rejection and warns about it, and slate reports it as the program's fault.
    handled: bool

    // Whether the program threw this promise away -- a call whose value was discarded, which is what
    // `main()` on a line of its own is.
    //
    // **This is what says a rejection can be reported NOW rather than at the end of the run.** The
    // question "was this handled?" normally cannot be answered when a promise fails, because `val p =
    // risky()` and `await p` three lines later is ordinary -- so the answer waits until everything has
    // settled. A promise nobody was given is the one case where it can be answered at once: there is
    // no name for it and no line that could await it, so the failure is final the moment it happens.
    //
    // Without this a fault inside a coroutine is silent for as long as the program lives, which for a
    // server is forever -- the diagnostic sits in `rejections` waiting for a drain that never comes,
    // and what the program looks like from outside is a hang.
    orphaned: bool

// A hold on a value that does not keep it alive.
//
// **One slot and nothing else**, so there is no finalizer: a `Value` carries no reference-counted
// member, which is what lets a zeroed payload be a valid one of these from the moment `alloc`
// answers -- a zeroed `Value` reads as `Null`, and `Null` is exactly what a cleared reference holds.
struct WeakRefObj
    target: Value

// A scope: the names written in one block, and the scope it sits inside.
struct EnvObj
    names: Map[string, Value]

    // Which names may be written to. A `val` is absent and a `var` is present.
    mutable: Map[string, bool]

    // Null at the top level, where there is nothing outside.
    parent: *EnvObj

// -- allocation ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// **A full heap answers null, and writing through that is a wild write.** The heap does not grow, so
// a program that outruns it has to be told rather than crashed: each of these collects first if the
// heap is nearly full, and `out_of_memory` reports the one case where that did not help.
//
// The collection here is safe for the same reason the one in `exec_block` is: these are only reached
// while the interpreter is between sub-expressions, with everything live either in a scope or on the
// shadow stack.

// Whether the last allocation failed is `current().heap_exhausted`. Read at the statement boundary,
// where there is a span to point at -- an allocator has no idea which line of the program it is
// serving.

// One object of every kind, taken while the heap is empty and never given back.
//
// **These are what a full heap is answered with, and without them it was answered with `null`.** The
// comment above has always said that writing through a full heap's answer is a wild write, and every
// one of the constructors below did exactly that: `alloc_or_collect` set `heap_exhausted` and
// returned null, and `new_str` wrote `o.s = s` straight through it. The flag is read at the *next*
// statement boundary, which the process never reached -- a slate program that outgrew the heap died
// with a bare SIGSEGV, no diagnostic, and none of the output it had already produced.
//
// A reserve is a real object in the heap with a real header, so it can be written to, traced and
// marked like any other. Handing the same one out twice makes two slate values share it, which is
// wrong and does not matter: the program stops at the end of the statement that got it.
// They are the ten `current().spare_*` fields -- see `vm_state.sysl`.

// Take the reserves. Called with an empty heap, where the allocation cannot fail.
//
// **The VM is a parameter because `new_vm` calls this on a VM nobody has entered yet.** A VM whose
// reserves were never taken holds ten nulls, and handing one of those out is the wild write the
// reserves exist to prevent: a second VM that filled its heap before anything had rebuilt it died
// with a bare SIGSEGV where the program's own VM said it had run out of memory.
//
// Not `private`: `new_vm` in `vm_state.sysl` takes a fresh VM's reserves here.
reserve_spares(v: *Vm)
    val h = &v.heap

    v.spare_str = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(h, sizeof(StrObj), &str_kind))
    v.spare_big = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(h, sizeof(BigObj), &big_kind))
    v.spare_array = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(h, sizeof(ArrayObj), &array_kind))
    v.spare_bytes = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(h, sizeof(BytesObj), &bytes_kind))
    v.spare_object = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(h, sizeof(ObjectObj), &object_kind))
    v.spare_closure = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(h, sizeof(ClosureObj), &closure_kind))
    v.spare_env = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(h, sizeof(EnvObj), &env_kind))
    v.spare_promise = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(h, sizeof(PromiseObj), &promise_kind))

    // **The weak pair's reserves are taken under kinds with NO weak hook, so they are STRONG**: the
    // weak map's under `object_kind`, the weak reference's under `held_ref_kind`. A reserve is handed
    // out only when the heap is full, and the statement that took it is the program's last -- so a
    // weak map that never drops an entry, or a weak reference that never clears, is exactly as good
    // for that one statement, and holding what it was given can only keep something alive, never
    // leave a slot pointing at freed storage. Each is still traced and finalized as the storage it
    // is: `object_kind` marks both halves of the table and `finalize_object` releases its `Buf`s, and
    // `held_ref_kind` marks the one `Value`, which owns nothing to release.
    //
    // **What it buys is that a program with no weak value has no weak object at all.** `sh.sysl.gc`
    // counts the live objects whose kind carries a `weaken` or an `ephemeron` hook and skips both
    // passes of a collection where the count is zero; a reserve under the weak kinds, alive for the
    // whole run, would hold both counts at one and every collection would walk them.
    // `tests_weak.sysl` pins both halves.
    v.spare_weak_map = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(h, sizeof(ObjectObj), &object_kind))
    v.spare_weak_ref = ptr_cast(gc.alloc(h, sizeof(WeakRefObj), &held_ref_kind))
    v.spare_cell = gc.alloc(h, sizeof(RangeObj), &cell_kind)
    forget_small_strs(v)

    // A zeroed payload is a valid object of each of these kinds except the ones holding a `Buf` or a
    // `Map`, which a tracer walks -- and a zero `Buf` is an empty one, so all ten are safe to trace
    // from the moment they exist.
    v.heap_exhausted = false
end reserve_spares

// Try once, collect, try again -- and answer the reserve rather than null when even that fails.
//
// **The caller is going to write through whatever comes back**, so this may not answer null. What it
// does instead is say so in `heap_exhausted`, which the statement boundary reports against a line.
// **The VM is a parameter and not asked for here**, for `charge_on`'s reason: the caller has already
// read it to find the reserve it passes, so asking again would be a second thread-local lookup on
// every allocation the interpreter makes.
alloc_or_collect(v: *Vm, size: usize, kind: *gc.Kind, spare: *u8) -> *u8
    var p = gc.alloc(&v.heap, size, kind)

    if p != null then return p

    collect_now()

    p = gc.alloc(&v.heap, size, kind)

    if p != null then return p

    v.heap_exhausted = true
    spare
end alloc_or_collect

// The same, over storage `sh.sysl.gc` has NOT cleared.
//
// **What a caller of this owes, and it is the whole of the difference**: every field the collector or
// the finalizer can read is written before the next allocation and before the next collection, and no
// reference-counted member is assigned into the block first. Assigning a `string`, a `Buf` or a `Map`
// releases whatever the bytes held before, which on a block off the free list is a dead object's
// field and on a fresh block is the host allocator's leavings -- so the release lands on something
// that is not a live reference and the process dies inside a tracer with no diagnostic.
//
// **That second condition is why `new_weak_ref` is the ONLY constructor here that takes this path.**
// Every other slate heap object owns sysl storage outside the heap -- a `StrObj` its `string`, an
// `ArrayObj` and a `BytesObj` their `Buf`, an `ObjectObj` two of them, a `ClosureObj` its parameter
// names, an `EnvObj` two `Map`s, a `PromiseObj` a message and a waiter list -- and that ownership is
// the bargain the finalizers are built on rather than an accident to be tidied away. A `WeakRefObj`
// is one `Value`, and a `Value` holds no counted member at all.
//
// **The reserve path is unchanged and must be**: a reserve is a real object that has been handed out
// before, so what comes back on an exhausted heap holds a live `Value` and is written over normally.
private alloc_or_collect_raw(v: *Vm, size: usize, kind: *gc.Kind, spare: *u8) -> *u8
    var p = gc.alloc_raw(&v.heap, size, kind)

    if p != null then return p

    collect_now()

    p = gc.alloc_raw(&v.heap, size, kind)

    if p != null then return p

    v.heap_exhausted = true
    spare
end alloc_or_collect_raw
// **Each of these charges what it was handed**, the object becoming the owner of storage that was a
// caller's local a moment ago. The mutators below charge what a regrow took; between them that is
// every byte a slate value holds outside the heap.
// **THE COUNT IS A PARAMETER SO THAT NO CALLER CAN FORGET IT**, which is why there is no
// `new_str(s)` to reach for: a string's length is O(1) only if every cell that is ever made carries
// the number, and a constructor that worked it out itself would scan text whose count the caller
// already knew. Nearly every string a program makes is derived from one it already had --
// concatenation adds two counts, a slice subtracts two positions, `repeat` multiplies -- and the
// count travels with it for nothing.
//
// `chars` must be the number of characters `s` holds. It is the caller's claim and nothing checks
// it, exactly as `new_array`'s `Buf` is; `new_str_scanned` is the way to say "I do not know".
//
// **The cursor starts at the front**, which is where a zeroed cell already is.
new_str_counted(s: string, chars: usize) -> *StrObj
    val v = current()
    var o: *StrObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(v, sizeof(StrObj), &str_kind, ptr_cast(v.spare_str)))

    o.s = s
    o.chars = chars
    o.cursor_char = 0
    o.cursor_byte = 0
    o.room = null
    charge_on(v, str_payload(o))
    o

// A cell for an integer that has outgrown 64 bits.
//
// **The `BigInt` is taken by value and this object becomes its owner**, which is `new_str`'s bargain
// -- the caller's copy dies with the caller's frame and the finalizer is what releases the limbs.
//
// **Nothing calls this directly but `big_value`**, which is what keeps the normalization invariant:
// a cell holding a number that fits a `long` would be a second spelling of an `Int`.
new_big(n: BigInt) -> *BigObj
    val v = current()
    var o: *BigObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(v, sizeof(BigObj), &big_kind, ptr_cast(v.spare_big)))

    o.n = n
    charge_on(v, big_payload(o))
    o

// A string whose count nobody knows, which is one walk of the bytes.
//
// **This is for text arriving from OUTSIDE the machine** -- a file, a socket, a child's output,
// decoded bytes, a database column -- where nothing upstream counted anything. Everywhere else the
// count is already in hand and `new_str_counted` is what says so.
new_str_scanned(s: string) -> *StrObj = new_str_counted(s, char_count(s))

new_array(items: Buf[Value]) -> *ArrayObj
    val v = current()
    var o: *ArrayObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(v, sizeof(ArrayObj), &array_kind, ptr_cast(v.spare_array)))

    o.items = items
    o.frozen = false
    charge_on(v, array_payload(o))
    o

// **The `Buf` is taken by value and this object becomes its owner**, which is `new_str`'s bargain: the
// caller's copy dies with the caller's frame and the finalizer is what takes the count to zero.
new_bytes(data: Buf[u8]) -> *BytesObj
    val v = current()
    var o: *BytesObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(v, sizeof(BytesObj), &bytes_kind, ptr_cast(v.spare_bytes)))

    o.data = data
    o.frozen = false
    charge_on(v, bytes_payload(o))
    o

new_object() -> *ObjectObj = new_object_on(current())

// The same, for a caller that already holds the VM -- an object literal, built once a turn by the
// instruction loop, which has it in hand. `charge_on` says why that matters: `current()` is a call
// into the dynamic loader on this platform, and a construction asked it once per step.
new_object_on(v: *Vm) -> *ObjectObj
    var o: *ObjectObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(v, sizeof(ObjectObj), &object_kind, ptr_cast(v.spare_object)))

    entries_fresh_on(v, o)

    // **A new table has no index and allocates none**, which `table.sysl`'s `SmallTable` is the
    // whole of: a lookup walks the entries until the table outgrows the small case, and an object
    // with two fields never does. An empty index is what says a table is in that case.
    o.index = buf()
    o.live = 0
    o.frozen = false
    o.data_value = false
    o.has_vals = false
    o.by_identity = false
    o.keys_bloom = 0
    charge_on(v, object_payload(o))
    o

// **A weak map is `new_object`'s twin under another kind**, and the two must be built alike --
// including the index it does not have.
new_weak_map() -> *ObjectObj
    val v = current()
    var o: *ObjectObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(v, sizeof(ObjectObj), &weak_map_kind, ptr_cast(v.spare_weak_map)))

    entries_drop(o)
    o.index = buf()
    o.live = 0
    o.frozen = false
    o.data_value = false
    o.has_vals = false
    o.by_identity = true
    o.keys_bloom = 0
    charge_on(v, object_payload(o))
    o

// **The one constructor that takes storage the allocator has not cleared**, `alloc_or_collect_raw`
// above saying why it is the only one. `target` is the whole payload and it is written on the very
// next line, so the only reader there is -- `weaken_weak_ref`, at a collection -- cannot see the
// block as it came back.
new_weak_ref(target: Value) -> *WeakRefObj
    val v = current()
    var o: *WeakRefObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect_raw(v, sizeof(WeakRefObj), &weak_ref_kind, ptr_cast(v.spare_weak_ref)))

    o.target = target
    o

new_closure(params: Buf[string], body: usize, scope: *EnvObj) -> *ClosureObj
    val v = current()
    var o: *ClosureObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(v, sizeof(ClosureObj), &closure_kind, ptr_cast(v.spare_closure)))

    o.params = params
    o.body = body
    o.scope = scope
    charge_on(v, closure_payload(o))
    o

new_promise() -> *PromiseObj
    val v = current()
    var o: *PromiseObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(v, sizeof(PromiseObj), &promise_kind, ptr_cast(v.spare_promise)))

    o.value = Null
    o.settled = false
    o.failed = false
    o.message = ""
    o.at = point(0)
    o.src = 0
    o.quit = false
    o.quit_status = 0
    o.waiters = buf()
    o.handled = false
    o.orphaned = false
    o

new_env(parent: *EnvObj) -> *EnvObj
    val v = current()
    var o: *EnvObj = ptr_cast(alloc_or_collect(v, sizeof(EnvObj), &env_kind, ptr_cast(v.spare_env)))

    o.names = map()
    o.mutable = map()
    o.parent = parent
    o

// -- growing a payload ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **A container that GROWS is where the charge would otherwise be missed**, and missing it is the
// same hole the constructors close wearing another hat: an array built by pushing a million elements
// is one small cell whose `Buf` has quietly taken sixteen megabytes. Each of these measures the
// capacity either side of the operation and charges the difference, which is nothing at all on the
// pushes that fit in storage the buffer already had.
//
// **`pop`, `remove`, `truncate` and `clear` are NOT here and need nothing**, a `Buf` keeping its
// storage when its count falls -- so a program that empties a buffer is still holding what it grew,
// and saying otherwise would report pages that are still committed as free.

// Append to a slate array.
array_push(o: *ArrayObj, v: Value)
    val was = o.items.cap()

    o.items.push(v)
    charge((o.items.cap() - was) * sizeof(Value))

// Put a value at a position, moving everything from there on up one.
array_insert(o: *ArrayObj, i: usize, v: Value)
    val was = o.items.cap()

    o.items.insert(i, v)
    charge((o.items.cap() - was) * sizeof(Value))

// Append one byte to a byte buffer.
bytes_push(o: *BytesObj, b: u8)
    val was = o.data.cap()

    o.data.push(b)
    charge(o.data.cap() - was)

// Append a run of bytes to a byte buffer.
bytes_extend(o: *BytesObj, bs: []const u8)
    val was = o.data.cap()

    o.data.extend(bs)
    charge(o.data.cap() - was)

// Hand a byte buffer's storage back, which is what `transfer` does to the sender's.
//
// **`clear` would not do it**: a `Buf` that is emptied keeps its storage, so a transferred buffer
// would read as empty to the program while still holding every page it grew.
bytes_release(o: *BytesObj)
    credit(bytes_payload(o))
    o.data = buf()

// Note that an object's two buffers have been rebuilt, which is what a rehash does to them.
object_recharged(o: *ObjectObj, was: usize)
    recharge(was, object_payload(o))

// Add a parked machine to what a promise will hand its answer to.
waiter_push(o: *PromiseObj, id: usize)
    val was = o.waiters.cap()

    o.waiters.push(id)
    charge((o.waiters.cap() - was) * sizeof(usize))

// Give a promise the complaint it failed with.
promise_says(o: *PromiseObj, message: string)
    credit(o.message.bytes.len)
    o.message = message
    charge(o.message.bytes.len)

// Note that a scope's tables have grown, which is what declaring a name into one may do.
env_recharged(o: *EnvObj, was: usize)
    recharge(was, env_payload(o))
